# Non-negotiable requirement: Device-agnostic player interfaces

Every player-facing interface in this application MUST be genuinely device-agnostic. This is a foundational product requirement, not a visual preference or a task to address after desktop development.

**Players must be able to participate fully using virtually any reasonably modern smartphone, tablet, or desktop browser, regardless of screen size, aspect ratio, or orientation.**

The player interface is mobile-first by definition. Desktop is simply another viewport in which it must work.

## 1. Design for the smallest practical viewport first

Use **320 CSS pixels wide as a required minimum**, and aim to support 280 pixels where practical.

Do not develop a layout at desktop dimensions and subsequently shrink it until it fits a phone. Design components so they naturally adapt to the space available.

At narrow widths, this may require fundamentally different arrangements of the same controls, not just smaller versions of the desktop layout.

## 2. Functionality takes precedence over layout consistency

Every action available to a player must remain accessible and usable on every supported viewport.

- No clipped, overlapping, obscured, or unreachable interactive elements.
- No horizontal page scrolling to reach essential controls.
- No buttons that become too small to tap reliably. Aim for 44 × 44 CSS pixel touch targets.
- No text shrinking to illegibility just to preserve a particular layout.
- No dependency on hover interactions, mouse input, or a physical keyboard.
- No critical controls hidden beneath sticky elements, browser chrome, or the on-screen keyboard.

Vertical scrolling is perfectly acceptable. **Making players scroll is preferable to making an interface unusable.**

## 3. Use responsive architecture, not viewport-specific patches

Audit the player UI for fixed widths, minimum widths, rigid grids, absolute positioning, overflow clipping, fixed heights, and other assumptions that prevent responsive behavior.

Replace those assumptions with flexible layouts, content-aware breakpoints, appropriate wrapping, and alternative arrangements where necessary.

Breakpoints should follow the needs of the content, not a handful of named phone models.

Do not fix individual screenshots by adding increasingly specific CSS overrides. Identify and resolve the underlying layout constraints.

Do not modify host, administrator, or public-display interfaces unless a shared component requires it.

## 4. Test actual gameplay, not just page rendering

A screen passing a DOM inspection is not enough. It must look correct, and a player must actually be able to use it.

Test all relevant player states, including joining, answering questions, betting, folding, manipulating cards, submitting answers, seeing results, and viewing leaderboards.

Test at viewport widths of 280, 320, 360, 375, 390, 430, 768, and 1280 CSS pixels, plus relevant landscape orientations.

Also test text enlargement, browser zoom, long content, on-screen keyboards, and dynamic state changes.

For each applicable viewport and game state, verify:

1. **Geometry:** No unintended overflow, clipping, overlap, or unreachable controls.
2. **Visibility:** Every necessary control and its label is actually visible and readable.
3. **Interaction:** Every necessary control can be operated successfully through its intended input method.

Use real browser rendering, screenshots, and interaction tests. Automated assertions alone do not prove visual usability.

## 5. Definition of done

A player-facing feature is NOT COMPLETE unless it functions correctly at the smallest supported viewport.

A fix that works at 390 pixels but fails at 320 pixels is not complete. A component that renders correctly but cannot be operated on a touchscreen is not complete.

If the interface cannot accommodate its content, redesign its presentation rather than compromising functionality.

When implementing or modifying any player-facing UI, proactively check all supported viewport sizes without waiting for me to report another mobile layout failure.

**This requirement applies permanently to all existing and future player-facing interfaces.**
